  "summary": "Recent preprints use polymer and generative models to infer cohesin kinetics and mitotic structural memory from chromosome folding data.",
  "synthesis": "Three preprints share a common approach: polymer or generative models infer organizing rules that are not directly observed, using chromosome structure as the readout. HiCONA, a generative Bayesian framework, infers hierarchical 3D genome organization and uses an Entropy Impact Score to identify features retained through mitosis, linking post-mitotic inheritance to hierarchical structural memory. A second preprint integrates biochemical kinetics with polymer modeling to infer cohesin turnover, motion, and encounter rules from sister chromatid folding signatures. A third generates chromosome conformational ensembles from epigenomic data that reproduce Hi-C maps and reveal folding landscapes across genetic and epigenetic contexts. What is new is the recovery of hidden parameters, including cohesin kinetic rules and hierarchical memory states, directly from structural readouts.",
